HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Thompson is a 49-year-old female who presents for a follow-up of her chronic problems. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately, and she is here to discuss this. She states that it is mostly in her elbows and it is really bothering her. She has been sitting at a desk more in the office and she does not know if this is being stagnant in that position all day has increased flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that. She reports that she is right-handed. She denies any other joint pain. She notes that sometimes she feels a little bit of numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation. She denies any other issues with her gout. Her last episode was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe. She reports that the medication that I gave her really controlled it, and she has not seen a flare-up since. She also denies any issues with her psoriasis. She reports that she has been under control. She reports that the clobetasol that she was given for her scalp has been doing a lot better.

