Janet Davis is a 33-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of right knee pain. She reports that the pain is bilateral and has been ongoing for the past 2 weeks. The onset of the pain began 2 weeks ago when she started doing more stair workouts. She thought this would help build muscle on her knees but it has not. The pain is equal in both knees. She denies any pain in other joints such as her elbows or shoulders. She had a right rotator cuff repair 2 years ago but has not had any issues since then. She denies any pins and needles in her feet, swelling in her legs, fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath. She does have high blood pressure but denies any elevation in blood pressure since the onset of her joint pain. She reports that her blood sugars have been under control. She is currently taking Norvasc 5 mg daily, which she has been taking every day.

Given the patient's current symptoms and examination findings, the diagnosis of bilateral knee pain, likely due to a strain, is made. We will send off an autoimmune panel to rule out any inflammatory arthropathy. She will see a physical therapist for further evaluation and treatment of her knee pain. I have also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with the pain. We will continue to monitor her blood pressure at home and have her send me a report in a couple of weeks. We will also order a lipid profile and continue her on metformin 500 mg twice a day.

