summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Christopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who presents to the clinic for a follow-up visit. He was initially seen in May 2020 for an initial evaluation of gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. At that time, we established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus and placed him on a spina catheter protocol. He underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed and programmed at a level of 2.0.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He notes that his walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which was not possible prior to surgery. He still has occasional times where he needs help. He also reports that his bladder control improved after surgery but has leveled off and he is now using adult diapers. He denies any issues with sores from the diaper or decreased or painful urination. He also denies headaches or pain where the shunt is.

The patient’s cognitive abilities have also improved. He reports that he does not feel lost when talking to someone anymore. His daughter reports that he is able to have a normal conversation with her again.

PAST HISTORY

Medical: Hypertension.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient resides in an assisted living facility. He reports that his daughter assists him with daily activities. He is able to ambulate with a walker.

MEDICATIONS

The patient is currently taking lisinopril 10 mg once daily.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Significant for impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss.
GU: Significant for urinary incontinence and urgency. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Significant for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

RESULTS

CT Scan of the head from 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

Vitals: Blood pressure is 124/80. Heart rate is 64. Respirations are 18. Weight is 174 pounds. Pain is 0 out of 10.

RESULTS OF TESTS

Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.

Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no-finger to nose.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

The patient is doing well. He has made significant improvement since shunt surgery. I do believe I see a tiny bit of blood fluid collection in the right parietal region. However, I do not believe that that is causing any symptoms and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the CT scan. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2 to 3 months before we go in changing it. If he is not having any headaches or pain and he has improved, that is all good for me. So, really there is nothing to worry about.

The patient does ask if he will be able to drive again. I told him that I knew he was going to ask me that one. I think at this point it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver safety evaluation. However, I do occasionally have patients who surprise me by improving over a 9 to 12 month range. So, he could definitely pass it at that point. So, if he improves and we can continue to talk about that then, I think it could be possible. He could definitely also enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. So, you could kind of do that preemptively and then we could continue to watch for his improvement as we go.

I am very pleased with how much he has improved. So, I'm sure when we talk again in 3 months, we will continue to be on that upward trajectory or improvement.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
