Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right upper quadrant pain that has been ongoing for several weeks. The patient has been advised to avoid high fat foods, but his symptoms have not improved. The ultrasound demonstrates gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but the gallbladder is inflamed. Given the patient's persistent symptoms and the lack of improvement with dietary modifications, I am recommending cholecystectomy.
• Medical Treatment: I am recommending a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This will involve several small incisions and insertion of a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder through one of the small incisions. The procedure will take approximately 1 hour to 1.5 hours. The patient will spend the night in the hospital for monitoring.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery. He should also eat high fiber foods. The patient was asked if he had any questions about the recommended treatment plan and he declined.
• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 1 week for post-operative visit. He should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat high fiber foods. He should also follow up with his primary care provider to continue managing his type 2 diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>
