ASSESSMENT

Right knee sprain. Right anterior cruciate ligament tear.

PLAN

Scott Johnson is a pleasant 38-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his right knee pain began 3 days ago when he was playing soccer. He reports that he felt a pop in his right knee and since then he has noticed some swelling. The swelling has gone down slightly, but he is still experiencing pain. The pain is ranging from 6/10 to 7/10 and is worse with ambulation. He denies any weight-bearing pain. He has been taking ibuprofen and it has helped some. He can bend his knee but it hurts towards the end of the motion. He can straighten his knee but it does not feel normal. He is able to bear weight on his leg but it feels loose. He is ambulating with a marked limp on his right leg.

I have reviewed the patient's examination and radiographic findings today. His examination is suggestive of an anterior cruciate ligament tear. I have recommended an MRI to evaluate the integrity of his ACL. He may need surgery but we will first take a look at the MRI results and reevaluate. I have recommended Meloxicam 50 mg once daily. He should ice the knee if he needs to. He should also use crutches when walking around. I advised him to moderate his activities and wear a knee immobilizer. He should limit his work activities. He can limit his work activities. He typically sits behind a desk but it is not been very comfortable sitting here lately. He is able to jog often. I advised him to slow that down for a while until we get the results of his MRI. He has a dog that enjoys playing outside but looks like will not be able to do that for a little bit. }}<|eot_id|>
