Patient reports he is a retired accountant and lives in a middle to upper middle class neighborhood. He is able to walk to the dining room but has difficulty walking further distances.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports he has a history of hypertension.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking lisinopril and atorvastatin.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain. Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Neurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations. Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure 124/80, heart rate 64, respirations 18, weight 174 pounds, pain 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Examination of the shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.

RESULTS

Recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge. He scored a 26 out of 30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25 out of 30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get 2 of the 3 memory words with cuing, and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling. At the time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice. Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.

Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face symmetric. No dysmetria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from non-nose to finger.

RESULTS

CT of the head 10/15/2020. By my reading there is a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's CT scan, we will leave the shunt set for another 2 to 3 months before we go in changing it. The patient is doing well and is able to walk to the dining room but has difficulty walking further distances. He is still incontinent of urine and is wearing a diaper. He is still experiencing some cognitive impairment. I will order him to return in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
