summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Rash.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports history of rosacea.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports she is not currently breastfeeding.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports history of taking clindamycin.

VITALS

Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV-V. Erythematous papules on the bilateral medial cheeks. Erythema around the nares.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Perioral dermatitis, rosacea, including ocular rosacea.

Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today for a follow-up of her perioral dermatitis. She has been on doxycycline 100 mg once daily for the past 2 weeks. She reports that she started using the doxycycline once a day as the pharmacist recommended she limit it to once a day as she noticed improvement. She notes that the improvement plateaued. She has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day, metro cream after washing her face, and no other cleansers or lotions. She is not currently breastfeeding. She is allergic to steroids.

Medical Treatment: I recommend that she apply the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash resolves. I also recommend that she continue to use the face wash once a day. She will discontinue the elidel cream. I will cancel the elidel cream and the clindamycin.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that doxycycline can cause upset stomach, heartburn, and make her more susceptible to sunburn. She is sun sensitive and is taking precautions. She was advised to use sunscreen. I explained that the doxycycline is low risk. I also advised that the doxycycline is not a steroid. She will continue to use the face wash once a day. She was advised that she may use a lotion to help moisturize her skin. I also explained that the tacrolimus ointment is not a steroid and will not cause thinning of the skin. She was advised that she may experience a tingling sensation 5-10 minutes after applying the cream, which is normal. I also advised that she may use the cream once a day if her skin becomes dry.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue to use the sulfacetamide wash once a day. Continue to use the metro cream. Increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day until the rash resolves. Once the rash resolves, continue to use doxycycline once a day for another week before stopping.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

Once the rash resolves, she may stop the tacrolimus ointment. She may continue to use doxycycline once a day for another week before stopping. She may continue to use the face wash and the metro cream.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

If her skin becomes dry, she may use the tacrolimus ointment for a couple of days. If her skin does not improve, she may restart the doxycycline.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

I will see her back in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
