Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jeremy Thompson is a pleasant 55-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. The patient reports that he injured his back approximately 4 months ago while moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm. He states that the pain is located in his lower back and is worse with sitting for a very short period of time. The patient describes the pain as severe and states that it feels like he is going to “crack in half.” He also reports that he has tingling in his right toes, which resolved after 1 hour. He denies any weakness in his legs. The patient states that the pain radiates into his right leg, but only if he is carrying something heavy. He denies any difficulty voiding or bowel movements. He states that he has tried Tylenol and ibuprofen, which only provide relief for a short period of time. He also states that he has tried heat and ice, but has not found relief. The patient denies any nausea or stomach upset with the use of ibuprofen.

