Heart rate: 68 beats per minute
Temperature: Afebrile
Oxygenation: 95% on room air

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck
• Examination: Bony protuberance of C5-C6.
• No pain to palpation.

Lumbar
• Examination: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.

RESULTS

X-ray of the lumbar spine was reviewed today. There was no acute bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Jeremy Thompson is a pleasant 55-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. He sustained an injury to his lower back 4 months ago while moving boxes for his son who was moving out of his college dorm. His symptoms include pain in his lower back, radiating into his right leg, and tingling in his right toes. He denies any weakness in his legs. He has tried Tylenol and ibuprofen, which only provide relief for a short period of time. He also denies any nausea or stomach upset with the use of ibuprofen.

I have ordered a lumbar spine MRI to get a better look at what is going on. I am going to continue with physical therapy and refer him to a physical therapist through our health system. I am going to prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with his pain.

If he has a herniated disk, we can discuss other treatment options such as an epidural steroid injection to help take down some of that inflammation. I want to see him back in 4 weeks to see how he is doing.

