summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that she had mentioned last time but we did not discuss it too much.

The patient reports she last drank on Monday. She had been drinking 4 to 5 times a week with hard alcohol. She notes she was taking the first dose of lisinopril and felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She felt much better and did not have the headaches or the whooshing that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know if it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.

She is taking her blood pressure medications. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or pressure or shortness of breath.

She is doing blood pressure checks at home. In the morning, she is seeing lower numbers than in the evening. In the evenings, if she sits there for a while and then takes it, it goes down. Kind of like when she comes here it is always high but then it goes down. In the mornings, it is generally more normal where it is supposed to be. She is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up instead of in the evening.

The patient reports she is not too far out of where she wants to be.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.
• Respiratory: Denies cough or shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or nausea.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle aches.
• Integumentary: Denies rash or itching.
• Neurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.
• Psychiatric: Denies anxiety or depression.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• General: Well appearing, pleasant, in no apparent distress.
• HEENT: No signs of swelling in the ankles.
• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation and percussion, unlabored breathing.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Musculoskeletal: No lower extremity edema.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient is drinking alcohol 4 to 5 days per week to excess. She reports she is taking her blood pressure medications. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or pressure or shortness of breath. She is doing blood pressure checks at home. She reports she is not too far out of where she wants to be.

I have discussed the potential medicine to help her with her alcohol use disorder. This medicine is called Naltrexone. She is interested in receiving the shot. I reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Her hypertension will likely improve completely with cessation of alcohol. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. She reports she may have taken this medicine in the past. If it was available in a pill form then she is almost certain she has tried it before.

She is currently taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Her target blood pressure is 130 to 140/70 to 80.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in week 6 for her second injection. I recommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix. She did defer this. }}<|eot_id|>
