Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for follow up of his right knee pain.

The patient reports his right knee pain has been ongoing for 2 months. He was previously seen in the clinic for this same issue and received a steroid injection. The injection provided relief for 2 weeks. The patient reports his pain is now at a 3 out of 10. The patient states his right knee is swollen. He denies any radiation of pain but reports his whole leg is swollen. The patient states his pain is worse when he has been sitting for a while and then he has to get up. He states he is stiff and has to use a cane to get his bearings. He reports his pain is somewhat better after a little activity. The patient reports he has been taking his medications as prescribed. He states he has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.
• Neurological: Reports stiff legs.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK:
Examination of the right knee: Range of motion slightly diminished. Palpation of the right knee and ankle: Some pitting. Capillary refill in the toes is normal. The right knee is warm to touch.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were ordered and are pending at this time.

ASSESSMENT

• Right knee pain.
• Edema of the right leg.

PLAN

The patient was given a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection today. The risks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia, among others. Verbal consent was attained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then bloody fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg of triamcinolone, and 1 mL of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post-procedure instructions were given. The patient will follow up with his PCP. I have recommended that the patient set up physical therapy and we will get an x-ray of his right knee.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
