Right upper quadrant pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Johnson is a pleasant 62-year-old male who presents today for evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. He was referred by Dr. Miller, who performed an ultrasound of his abdomen. The patient reports that his symptoms started several weeks ago. He notes that his pain is located on the right-hand side of his belly and is located underneath his ribs. The pain is described as a squeezing pain that comes and goes. He adds that when his pain is severe, he gets nauseated and sick to his stomach. The patient reports that his pain is worse after meals, both immediately after eating and a couple of hours after eating. The patient notes that he likes to eat fatty foods, which he believes may be contributing to his symptoms.

The patient is a type 2 diabetic who was previously advised to manage his condition with diet and exercise. He has since lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months and has noticed an improvement in his blood sugars. He has been eating a low-carb diet and has been doing well.

The patient reports that he has a family history of gallbladder disease. Both his father and mother had their gallbladders removed.

Regarding his activity level, the patient reports that his pain limits his ability to exercise. However, he enjoys photography and likes to get out and take nature pictures. He notes that he likes to get down near the river, which is where he likes to catch fish. He has not been to the new park behind the Rex Center.

The patient denies fevers, but notes that he has had low-grade fevers. He also denies vomiting.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers.
• Gastrointestinal: Endorses right upper quadrant pain, nausea.
• Psychiatric: Endorses anxiety related to his symptoms.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, rubs, or murmurs noted.
• Abdominal exam: Positive bowel sounds. Soft and non-distended abdomen. Positive guarding to the right upper quadrant. Mild tenderness to palpation of the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with symptoms consistent with cholecystitis with gallstones. The patient has had a history of gallstones and has been advised to avoid fatty foods. He has had no improvement in his symptoms despite dietary modifications.
• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that we proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The patient understands and agrees with this plan.
• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of the procedure and the risks and benefits in detail. The patient understands the risks and benefits of the procedure.

2. Type 2 diabetes.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently being managed with diet and exercise. He has been doing well and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months.
• Medical Treatment: The patient should follow up with his primary care physician to monitor his diabetes. I will continue to monitor his diabetes as outlined by his endocrinologist or primary care provider.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week. He will be discharged to go home and can resume regular activities. He should avoid heavy lifting and strenuous exercise for the first 24 hours after the procedure. He should be able to walk around the house and get out of bed the day after surgery. He should be able to return to work in 1-2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
