summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his hypertension and palpitations. He states that he was recently in the emergency room for high blood pressure and palpitations. He reports that he had a few cups of coffee and did not eat breakfast on that day and thinks it was probably a panic attack. He has a history of hypertension. He denies any episodes since that time.

The patient reports that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He states that he was recently prescribed a new medication by his primary care physician and has been taking it for about a month. He is currently taking a combination of benicar hct, 40-25mg daily. He is also taking amlodipine, 10mg daily. He states that he is checking his blood pressure at home daily and reports that it has been running 146-155mmHg. He states that he is trying to reduce his salt intake and is doing well with it. He reports that he tries to walk on the treadmill when he can.

MEDICATIONS

Benicar HCT 40-25mg daily.
Amlodipine 10mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, dizziness.
• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea on exertion.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• CVS: Auscultation of the heart reveals a regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

RESULTS

Thyroid profile from 03/01/2021 shows normal thyroid function.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his hypertension and palpitations. He is a new patient to our clinic. He was recently in the emergency room for high blood pressure and palpitations. He reports that he had a few cups of coffee and did not eat breakfast on that day and thinks it was probably a panic attack. He has a history of hypertension. He denies any episodes since that time.

The patient reports that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He states that he was recently prescribed a new medication by his primary care physician and has been taking it for about a month. He is currently taking a combination of benicar hct, 40-25mg daily. He is also taking amlodipine, 10mg daily. He states that he is checking his blood pressure at home daily and reports that it has been running 146-155mmHg. He states that he is trying to reduce his salt intake and is doing well with it. He reports that he tries to walk on the treadmill when he can.

Mr. Roberts is currently on three medications and has reached maximum dose. We discussed the addition of spironolactone to his regimen. He understands the potential side effects of this medication including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium levels. We will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in two weeks. If he is not controlled on spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic. They have access to newer procedure-based technologies that can lower his blood pressure without medication, including renal denervation.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
