summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

IBS with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting. She was last seen in August 2019. She reports she has been having intermittent episodes of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. She states that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has been having spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She denies that she has been taking her fiber supplement, citrucel, regularly.

The patient states that she has been having diarrhea, cramping, and abdominal pain. She reports that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she had some fried chicken today and it was okay.

She reports that she has been having a bowel movement 2 to 3 times a day. She states that she has formed stools. She reports that she has diarrhea when she goes out. She denies that it is the food or the alcohol, but she states that she does not like to drink.

PAST HISTORY

Medical: She has a history of IBS.

MEDICATIONS

She reports that she was prescribed Reglan and was instructed to take it as needed for nausea. She reports that she has not been taking it. She was also prescribed Bentyl, which she was taking 4 times a day for cramping. She was also advised to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, and Bentyl up to 4 times a day for intestinal cramping. She was advised to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers or chills.
Eyes: Denies eye pain.
Ears, Nose, Mouth, Throat: Denies ear pain, nasal congestion, sore throat.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Gastrointestinal: Reports diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain.
Genitourinary: Denies urinary incontinence or difficulty with urination.
Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.
Neurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

Constitutional: In no acute distress.
Neurological: Afebrile. Cranial exam: Normal. Motor exam: Normal. Reflexes: Normal.

RESULTS

The patient's physical exam today shows that she is afebrile. Her abdomen is soft, non-tender to palpation. No masses or organomegaly appreciated. Active bowel sounds appreciated.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting. She was last seen in August 2019. She reports she has been having intermittent episodes of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. She states that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has been having spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She denies that she has been taking her fiber supplement, citrucel, regularly.

The patient reports that she has been having diarrhea, cramping, and abdominal pain. She reports that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she had some fried chicken today and it was okay.

She reports that she has been having a bowel movement 2 to 3 times a day. She states that she has formed stools. She reports that she has diarrhea when she goes out. She denies that it is the food or the alcohol, but she states that she does not like to drink.

The patient is a pleasant female with IBS and functional abdominal pain, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits. I have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications, as well as starting a neuromodulator such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I have asked her to follow up in 1 month by emailing over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed. }}<|eot_id|>
