summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Adult hydrocephalus.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control for 8-10 years. He has been followed by Dr. Smith since May 2020. He underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020, with a Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration set at 2.0.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Reports urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies abdominal pain or discomfort.
Genitourinary: Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80. Heart rate: 64. Respirations: 18. Weight: 174 pounds. Pain: 0 out of 10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Pleasant and cooperative. Cranial nerves intact. EOMs intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from nose to finger.

RESULTS

CT scan of the head 10/15/2020: The frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro is 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

The patient is a pleasant and cooperative male who is able to converse easily. The shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.0. He was tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge. He scored 26 out of 30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25 out of 30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get 2 of the 3 memory words with cuing and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling. And at the time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice.

Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18-20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17 out of 28.

Cranial nerves, pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from nose to finger. Of note, the CT scan from the head of 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

The patient's shunt setting was reviewed and is currently set at 2.0. I do believe I see a tiny bit of blood fluid collection in the right parietal region. However, I do not believe that that is causing any symptoms and it actually was not documented by the radiologist when he read the CT scan. Given the patient is not experiencing any headaches or pain and has improved, I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we go in changing it.

The patient does have a question about driving. I told him that at this point it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver's safety evaluation. However, I do occasionally have patients who surprise me by improving over a 9-12 month range. And that definitely could be him. You could definitely pass it at that point. So if you improve and we can continue to talk about that then, you know, I think it could be possible, and I'd be happy to recommend it. Now you could definitely also enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. So you could kind of do that preemptively, and then we could kind of watch for your improvement as we go.

I'm very pleased with how much he has improved. I'm sure when we talk again in 3 months, we'll continue to be on that upward trajectory or improvement. }}<|eot_id|>
