ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Patient is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. She reports she is drinking alcohol four to five times per week. She is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg oral daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg oral daily. She is compliant with her blood pressure medications. Her blood pressure is 150/100. She is interested in stopping her alcohol use.

We discussed the option of Vivitrol. She has tried Naltrexone in the past in pill form, but she agrees that she would be more compliant with the shot. We discussed the benefits of alcohol cessation, including improved blood pressure control. We discussed potential side effects of Vivitrol, including nausea, headache, dizziness, anxiety, fatigue, and insomnia, which are generally mild and resolve within a few days. She is interested in starting the injectable form of Naltrexone. We discussed the risks and benefits and she understands.

She will receive her first dose on May 17th. We will see her back in a month for her second dose. We will review her blood pressure and assess her progress.

