summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Pain in the right upper quadrant.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Smith is a 64-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The pain is located in the right upper quadrant and is associated with nausea. The pain is intermittent and has been ongoing for several weeks. The patient states that it is severe and is associated with a feeling of nausea. He denies vomiting. The patient states that he has had a history of diabetes and was diagnosed 2 years ago. He states that he has been following a low-carb diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months.

The patient reports that his symptoms have been persistent and has been experiencing pain, nausea, and vomiting. He denies any fevers. The patient states that he has a family history of gallstones. He reports that his father and mother both had gallstones and had to have their gallbladder removed.

The patient is currently taking Lipitor, Aspirin, and Metformin. He reports that he is allergic to sulfonamides.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient is married and has 2 children. He is a retired construction worker and enjoys photography.

MEDICATIONS

Lipitor 40 mg PO daily
Aspirin 81 mg PO daily
Metformin 1000 mg PO twice daily

VITALS

Blood pressure: 128/88 mmHg
Respiratory rate: 16
Pulse rate: 68
Temperature: Afebrile
Oxygen saturation: 99% on room air

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.

Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

Abdomen: Soft, nontender, and nondistended. Positive bowel sounds. Mild guarding to palpation in the right upper quadrant. Murphy's sign is positive. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones are present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a history of right upper quadrant pain and nausea. The patient has a history of diabetes and has been following a low-carb diet. He has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. The patient reports that he has had a family history of gallstones. The patient's symptoms have been persistent and have not improved with dietary modifications. The patient's abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones in the gallbladder and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.
- Medical Treatment: Given the patient's persistent symptoms and lack of improvement with dietary modifications, we will proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The patient will be advised to avoid high-fat foods after surgery and follow a diet high in fiber.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

1. Continue with a low-carb diet.
2. Avoid high-fat foods after surgery.
3. Follow a diet high in fiber.
4. Follow up in 1 week for post-op check.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended instructions. }}<|eot_id|>
