Right shoulder pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Matthew Jackson is a 67-year-old male who presents today for evaluation of right shoulder pain. The pain began 4 days ago. He reports that he was playing tackle football and was tackled, landing on his right shoulder. He has been having pain since then. He states that he is right handed. He denies numbness or tingling in his fingers. He is able to lift his arm but states that he has to avoid lifting anything heavy. He reports that he has been taking Tylenol for the pain. He denies that the Tylenol has helped. He states that he is able to lift his arm to his ear. He reports that he is able to do all activities of daily living.

The patient has a history of appendicitis and had an appendectomy 3-4 years ago.

MEDICATIONS

Tylenol.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 122/76. Heart rate: 82. Oxygenation: 95% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: In general in good condition.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the cervical spine reveals no tenderness to palpation. Full range of motion. Examination of the right shoulder reveals pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right shoulder, obtained today, shows an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Right acromioclavicular joint fracture.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right shoulder pain. The patient reports that he was playing tackle football and was tackled, landing on his right shoulder. He has been having pain since then. His x-ray today shows an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. I have reviewed the x-ray with the patient and explained the findings. I have recommended a sling for support. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have ordered a CT scan of the right shoulder to get a better look at the joint. I have referred the patient to physical therapy to strengthen the shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week to check on his progress. He will continue to wear the sling for support. He will take meloxicam 15 mg once a day. He will follow up with me in 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>
