Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Johnson is a 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. He reports that he injured his knee approximately 2.5 weeks ago while stepping off a curb. He denies falling down at the time of the injury and states that he turned over his ankle. He reports that the pain is located on the outside of his right knee. The patient states that the pain has been persistent and has been associated with clicking. He denies numbness or tingling in his foot. He reports that he has a history of numbness and tingling in his right foot due to a previous fracture of his second toe. He denies any significant trauma to his right knee prior to this injury. The patient reports that he has been taking ibuprofen and has been able to work through the pain. He reports that he is able to ambulate without a limp and has full range of motion. He reports that his symptoms have remained constant over the past 2 weeks.

The patient reports that he is active and rides a bike. He reports that he is able to do squats but notes that when he goes all the way down into a squat, he feels a clicking sensation. He denies any significant pain with ambulation. He reports that he has been icing his knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off, 4 times a day. He notes that this has provided minimal relief.

MEDICATIONS

Meloxicam 15 mg once a day.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In general appearance well-nourished.
CV: Auscultation of the heart is normal. Auscultation of the lungs is clear to auscultation bilaterally. Extremities: No clubbing or cyanosis. No edema. No significant lymphadenopathy. Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right knee reveals slight edema. There is a slight effusion. No pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full range of motion. Negative Lachman sign. Examination of the lower extremities reveals a palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were reviewed and are negative for acute fracture or bony abnormality.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Lateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee.

The patient is a 45-year-old male who presents with right knee pain. He reports that he injured his knee approximately 2.5 weeks ago while stepping off a curb. He denies falling down at the time of the injury and states that he turned over his ankle. He reports that the pain is located on the outside of his right knee. The patient states that the pain has been persistent and has been associated with clicking. He denies numbness or tingling in his foot. He reports that he has a history of numbness and tingling in his right foot due to a previous fracture of his second toe. He denies any significant trauma to his right knee prior to this injury. The patient reports that he has been taking ibuprofen and has been able to work through the pain. He reports that he is able to ambulate without a limp and has full range of motion. He reports that his symptoms have remained constant over the past 2 weeks.

The patient reports that he is active and rides a bike. He reports that he is able to do squats but notes that when he goes all the way down into a squat, he feels a clicking sensation. He denies any significant pain with ambulation. He reports that he has been icing his knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off, 4 times a day. He notes that this has provided minimal relief.

I have reviewed the results of the right knee x-ray and they do not show any acute fracture or bony abnormality which is not surprising based on his injury.

I have recommended the patient undergo a physical therapy referral to strengthen the muscles around his knee. I have also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have advised him that if his symptoms do not improve, we may need to consider an MRI.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks to assess his symptoms.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
