Right heel pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Dennis Smith is a 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right heel pain. He is a triathlete and has been training for the last 3 months. He reports that he has been running 10 kilometers in the morning, cycling for 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swimming 30 minutes to an hour in the evening 5 days a week. He states that the pain started 2 weeks ago and has been persistent since then. He describes the pain as a dull ache that is worse with activity and better with rest. He states that he has difficulty walking and standing after prolonged periods of sitting.

The patient denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in his right leg. He denies any trauma to the area. He states that he is not taking any medications at this time.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

Neurological: Examination of the lower extremities is normal. No edema or erythema noted.

MUSCULOSKELETAL

Examination of the right lower extremity reveals tenderness to palpation over the right Achilles tendon. No edema or erythema noted. Strength is 5/5 in the right calf. The Thompson test is negative.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right ankle are not obtained at this time.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Achilles tendinitis.

The patient is a 42-year-old male who presents with right heel pain. He is a triathlete and has been training for the last 3 months. He reports that the pain started 2 weeks ago and has been persistent since then. He describes the pain as a dull ache that is worse with activity and better with rest. He states that he has difficulty walking and standing after prolonged periods of sitting.

I have reviewed the patient's examination findings and medical history today. The patient's physical examination reveals tenderness to palpation over the right Achilles tendon. The Thompson test is negative.

Given the patient's symptoms and examination findings, I have diagnosed him with Achilles tendinitis. The patient is advised to stop training for 2 weeks to allow the inflammation to resolve. I have recommended that he take ibuprofen 3 tablets every 6 hours for pain and inflammation. The patient is also advised to follow up with physical therapy for 2 sessions to strengthen and stretch the calf muscles.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient is advised to stop training for 2 weeks to allow the inflammation to resolve. He is also advised to take ibuprofen 3 tablets every 6 hours for pain and inflammation. He is advised to follow up with physical therapy for 2 sessions to strengthen and stretch the calf muscles. He is to return to clinic in 7 to 10 days for a follow-up appointment. }}<|eot_id|>
