HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christina Rodriguez is a 65-year-old female with a past medical history significant for type 2 diabetes mellitus who presents to the clinic today for a nonhealing foot ulcer.

The patient reports that the ulcer has been present for approximately 1 year. She denies any trauma to the foot and states that the ulcer just seemed to appear. She notes that she has been on antibiotics for the past 5 months, specifically cephalexin, but the ulcer has not improved. The patient states that she has been having a lot of drainage from the ulcer and is feeling more tired. She denies pain but notes that she has decreased sensation in her right foot. She reports that she will be walking around the zoo this weekend.

The patient states that her diabetes is not well controlled. She reports that her last A1c was 10. She reports that she is currently taking metformin and insulin. She notes that she has been eating out and not adhering to her diet. She denies taking any other medications.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient denies smoking and alcohol use.

VITALS

Her vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the right foot shows a 2 cm x 1 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There is yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. There is no necrosis. There is no bony exposure. The wound does not appear to be painful.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right foot show no evidence of osteomyelitis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 65-year-old female with a nonhealing foot ulcer. The patient is a diabetic and has had this ulcer for approximately 1 year. She has been on antibiotics for the past 5 months. She denies pain but notes that she has decreased sensation in her right foot. She reports that she will be walking around the zoo this weekend. She reports that her diabetes is not well controlled.

I have reviewed the patient's X-rays and they show no evidence of osteomyelitis, which is good.

I have reviewed the patient's vital signs and they are within normal limits.

I have reviewed the patient's examination and she has a 2 cm x 1 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There is yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. There is no necrosis. There is no bony exposure. The wound does not appear to be painful.

The patient's ABI is pending.

I have reviewed the patient's radiographic results and they show no evidence of osteomyelitis.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. I will perform another debridement of the ulcer at that time. I have prescribed her Colaganase ointment, which she will apply to the wound once daily and cover with a sterile dressing. She will continue this until we see the wound start to shrink. We will also get her a surgical shoe and she will wear that to help take pressure off the area.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
