summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julia Smith is a pleasant 55-year-old female who presents for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction. She reports that she had a surprise episode of chest pain and shortness of breath. She was admitted to the hospital and underwent an angioplasty with stent placement. She reports that she has been doing well since the procedure, but she is here today for a follow-up visit. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath today. She states that she has been able to continue to walk her dog, but she has noticed that her legs are a bit swollen since she was discharged from the hospital. She reports that she is able to sleep flat at night, but she has had to use a few pillows. She states that she is able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She reports that she is not watching her salt intake as closely as she should be, but she does eat vegetables on her pizza. She states that she has been watching her blood sugars and is trying to manage her diabetes. She denies any leg swelling and is able to ambulate. She reports that she is currently taking metformin and is trying to stay active.

MEDICATIONS

She is currently taking aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, Toprol 50 mg daily, and metformin.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.
Cardiovascular: Reports chest pain and shortness of breath.
Respiratory: Denies dyspnea.
Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.
Genitourinary: Denies urinary incontinence or urinary retention.
Musculoskeletal: Reports leg swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck: No jugular venous distention. No carotid bruits.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
CV: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.
Extremities: 1+ pitting edema of the right lower extremity. No distal pulses. No capillary refill.
Musculoskeletal: No clubbing or cyanosis.

RESULTS

EKG: Normal sinus rhythm with a good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes are anticipated after a STEMI.

RESULTS FROM OTHER SOURCES

Echo: Reduced ejection fraction of 35%.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Ms. Smith is a 55-year-old female who presents for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction. Her primary and first diagnosis is coronary artery disease. She has a history of coronary artery disease. We will continue her aspirin 81 mg daily and brilinta 90 mg twice daily. We will continue her Lipitor 80 mg daily and Toprol 50 mg daily. We will refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to increase her activity level. Her second diagnosis is newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation. We will continue her lisinopril 20 mg daily. Considering that she is retaining fluid, we will prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. We will add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. We will repeat another echo in about 2 months. Her third diagnosis is hypertension. Her blood pressure is fine, and she is tolerating her medications. We will add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. We will consider nutrition counseling for diet modifications. She will repeat her labs. }}<|eot_id|>
