summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right hand pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Alexis Wilson is a 34-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand pain. She states that she has been experiencing numbness and tingling in her right hand. She notes that she sits at a keyboard all day and has been having some issues for 6 months. The numbness and tingling have been more persistent over the last month. She denies pain at rest. She reports that the pain is worse when she is typing on the keyboard. She states that she has been taking ibuprofen and that not typing on the keyboard helps. She also notes that her mouse use worsens her symptoms. She denies pain or tingling at night.

The patient reports that she is a right-handed individual and has been using her computer for 6 months. She reports that she has been taking ibuprofen for the pain. She has tried not typing on the keyboard and using her mouse less frequently. She denies any recent injuries.

MEDICATIONS

Ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain.
Neurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the right hand.

VITALS

Not obtained.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Examination of the right upper extremity reveals decreased grip strength. Examination of the right wrist reveals pain with extension and flexion. Positive Tinel's sign.

RESULTS

EMG and nerve conduction studies have been ordered.

ASSESSMENT

1. Right carpal tunnel syndrome.

PLAN

The patient was advised to wear a wrist splint at night. She was prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg, 4 times a day. She was advised to continue using the ibuprofen. We discussed the possibility of an EMG and nerve conduction studies. We also discussed the possibility of physical therapy and surgery if necessary. We will follow up in 2 weeks to review the EMG and nerve conduction studies.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient was advised to follow up in 2 weeks to review the EMG and nerve conduction studies. }}<|eot_id|>
