summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right ankle pain.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports a history of playing soccer. Denies a history of ankle injuries.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he is married and has children. His children watch him play soccer. He has a friend who was visiting him at the time of the injury.

CURRENT ILLNESS

Patient presents with right ankle pain and swelling after falling on the ice. He states that he was going out to take off the trash and he must have slipped on a patch of ice. He reports that he was careful, but he slipped and fell. He was helped up by a friend who was visiting him. He states that he was unable to walk on it initially, but last night he was able to put a little bit more weight on it. He reports that he is still limping. He states that he has been keeping it elevated and icing it. He also took some ibuprofen, which has helped.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 120/80 mmHg.
Temperature: 98.6°F.
Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute.
Pulse: 80 beats per minute.
Oxygen saturation: 99% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In general, he appears well.
CV: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Neuro: Normal sensation to light touch in all extremities. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse.
Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle reveals ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. There is tenderness to palpation and anterolaterally in the soft tissue. There is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right ankle is reviewed and is negative for fracture.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Right ankle sprain.

Patient is advised to keep the leg elevated, especially when seated, and to continue to ice the ankle. He is given an aircast and crutches for the next 1-2 days. He is advised to start walking on it as tolerated.

Patient's symptoms should significantly improve in the next 2 weeks. I will follow up with him then and see how he is doing. He is advised to continue taking nsaids as needed for pain.

INSTRUCTIONS

Follow-up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
