summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Elevated cholesterol.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary Hispanic female who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. She is a healthy young adult who was recently screened for hyperlipidemia. She states that she had a finger prick test done during her annual checkup, which revealed elevated cholesterol levels. She denies any significant family history of hyperlipidemia. She notes that she enjoys riding her bike and has a balanced diet. She does not recall any family members with premature heart disease or sudden death. She does have a cousin who died at the age of 44 and they think that he had a heart attack. She denies a history of smoking. She is currently living on her own and does not have any friends who smoke.

PAST HISTORY

She reports that she has no significant past medical history. She has had no previous diagnoses or surgeries.

SOCIAL HISTORY

She is currently living on her own and does not have any friends who smoke. She enjoys riding her bike and going outside. She likes to cook and eat a variety of foods. She has had no recent travel.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that she is not currently taking any medications.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or wheezing.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.
• Skin: Denies rash or itching.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• General Examination: In no acute distress.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Skin: No rashes or lesions.
• Neck: No thyroid enlargement.

RESULTS

Cholesterol level was elevated at 210 mg/dL.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Hyperlipidemia.
• The patient was advised to follow up with a nutritionist for dietary modifications.
• She was advised to continue being active and exercise regularly, such as riding her bike.
• We will order a full cholesterol panel, glucose, a complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels.
• Genetic testing was also ordered to rule out familial hyperlipidemia.
• We will follow up in 3-6 months after the patient has met with the nutritionist and instituted dietary changes.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with a nutritionist for dietary modifications. She will continue to be active and exercise regularly, such as riding her bike. We will order a full cholesterol panel, glucose, a complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. Genetic testing was also ordered to rule out familial hyperlipidemia. We will follow up in 3-6 months after the patient has met with the nutritionist and instituted dietary changes. }}<|eot_id|>
