summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Low back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Smith is a 32-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. He states that his symptoms started approximately 2 weeks ago. He was lifting a box of books when he felt a "pop" and he has been experiencing pain since. The pain is described as achy and sharp in his low back. He states that the pain radiates down his left leg. He denies any numbness or tingling in his legs. He states that he doesn't have any loss of sensation. He denies any weakness in his legs. He denies any loss of bladder or bowel control.

The patient reports that the pain has been getting worse over the past 2 weeks. He states that he has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for pain relief, which sometimes helps. He denies any history of spine surgery. He reports that he has a family history of back pain. His father has back pain.

The patient reports that he is an Olympic weightlifter. He states that he has a sedentary job. He denies that the pain has affected his job. He reports that he is inconsistent with his exercise. He states that he is a fan of the Tatum Hotspur. He denies playing sports. He states that he played soccer in the past.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a family history of back pain.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he is a smoker.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he takes ibuprofen and Tylenol.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.
• Respiratory: Denies any shortness of breath.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
• Neurological: Reports low back pain with radiculopathy.

VITALS

Blood pressure is 130/70. Respiratory rate is 18.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In general, he appears well-nourished.

Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.

Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

Musculoskeletal: There is midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Supine straight leg test is positive.

NEUROLOGIC
• Motor: No weakness.
• Reflexes: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.
• Sensory: No decreased sensation.

RESULTS

An MRI of the lumbar spine was obtained today. The results show a disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

