summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension and palpitations.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today with hypertension and palpitations.

The patient reports that he was in the emergency room recently with high blood pressure and palpitations. He notes that he consumed a few cups of coffee and had no breakfast, and he thinks that it was more of a panic attack than anything else. He has not felt it again since. He also reports that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of high blood pressure. He notes that he thought it was getting better, and he has a new primary doctor who put him on a new medication about a month ago. He reports that he is taking Benicar HCT, 40/25 mg daily, and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He is checking his blood pressure at home daily and notes that it has been running 146 to 155.

The patient reports that he is trying to reduce his salt intake. He notes that he is doing well with it, not eating too much salt. He also reports that he is trying to walk on the treadmill when he can.

The patient reports that he has been checked for potassium levels one week ago and his level was 4.0. He has not had it checked since.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of: Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Regular rate and rhythm. No chest pain.

RESULTS

Potassium 4.0 mg/dL (reference range: 3.5-5.1 mg/dL).

ASSESSMENT

Hypertension.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's medical history and current medications, I discussed the importance of reducing his salt intake and increasing his potassium levels. I advised him to follow the DASH diet. I also recommended that he exercise for 30 minutes most days a week, which could potentially lower his blood pressure by 7-10 points. I discussed the importance of getting his blood pressure under control to prevent complications such as heart failure and kidney disease.

The patient is currently on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. His blood pressure is not under control. I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. I advised him that we will need to monitor his potassium and sodium levels. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. If his blood pressure is not under control on Spironolactone, I will refer him to the resistant hypertension clinic. In the resistant hypertension clinic, they will look into secondary causes of his hypertension. They have access to newer procedure-based technologies that can lower his blood pressure without medication, such as renal denervation. I advised him of the potential side effects of Spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. I also advised him that the next medication would be carvedilol, which I try to avoid giving as it has potential for erectile dysfunction. I advised him that we will cross that road when we need to.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
