summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. She has a history of significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on 04/16/2021 her blood pressure was 130 over 90. After presenting at 150 over 100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports that she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that was mentioned last time but we did not get to discuss it too much.

The patient reports she last had a drink on Monday. She has gone a couple days without it. She has been drinking for a while. She drinks four to five times a week hard alcohol.

She is taking her blood pressure medications. She is only taking half of the lisinopril. The first time she took it she felt lethargic and tired so she only took a half dose the next time. She felt much better and she did not have the headaches or the "swishing" that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know if it is okay until she stops drinking.

She is also taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies any chest tightness or pressure or any shortness of breath.

She is doing blood pressure checks at home. She is seeing lower numbers in the morning than in the evening. She says in the morning she is seeing 130 to 145 over 95 to 100. She says in the evenings if she sits there for a while and then takes it it goes down. She says it is generally more normal in the morning. She is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up instead of in the evening.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills.
• Respiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, sore throat.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest tightness or pressure.
• Psychiatric: Denies anxiety or depression.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No cva tenderness.

VITALS REVIEWED.

RESULTS

• Blood pressure: Essentially unchanged.
• CBC and CMP: Essentially unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. She is struggling with alcohol use disorder. She has been drinking four to five times a week to excess. She is not a smoker. She is not diabetic. She is not using any illicit substances.

We discussed the benefits of stopping drinking. She would like to start Naltrexone. She is interested in the injectable form. I think the injectable form is the best option. She is willing to start on 05/17/2021. We discussed side effects. I will see her back in 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. I also recommended upgrading her influenza vaccination and Shingrix. She is deferring this.

The patient is currently taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Her target blood pressure is 130 to 140 over 70 to 80. }}<|eot_id|>
