summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee swelling.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a 70-year-old male who presents today for an injection to the right knee. He was last seen in clinic in 2021 and received a right knee steroid injection. The patient reports that the injection provided him with 2 weeks of relief. The patient describes that his right knee has been swollen for approximately 2 months. He states that the swelling has spread down his leg, and he is now having pain in his right leg. The patient states that he is using compression socks to help with the swelling, but it has not been effective. He rates his pain level as 6 out of 10 on a normal day, but today it is 3 out of 10. The patient states that he is experiencing stiffness in his right knee, especially when he has been sitting for a while and then tries to get up. He states that he has been using a cane to help him get his bearings. He reports that he is still able to ambulate, but it is a bit difficult. The patient states that he is taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily, which he has been taking for approximately 2 months. He reports that he has been taking his medications regularly.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the right knee: Examination of the right leg shows trace pitting in the knee and ankle. Capillary refill is normal. The knee is hot to the touch. Range of motion is slightly diminished. The patient reports pain with straightening of the knee.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were taken today and will be reviewed.

ASSESSMENT

1. Right knee arthritis.
2. Swelling in the right leg.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and medical history today, we discussed the nature of his current symptoms and treatment options. We discussed that the right knee steroid injection provided him with 2 weeks of relief in 2021. I have recommended that we give him another right knee steroid injection today. I also recommended that we obtain an x-ray of the right knee to assess for any bony abnormalities that may be contributing to his current symptoms. We discussed the risks of the procedure, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. The patient was advised of these risks and provided verbal consent. We discussed that we will also order an x-ray of the right leg to assess for any edema. We discussed that the edema in the right leg may be related to his salt intake and that he should try to reduce his salt intake. We also discussed that he should prop his feet up as much as possible, especially at night. I advised that if these measures do not provide relief, he should contact his primary care provider. I also advised that he should continue taking his Xeljanz and Celebrex daily.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care provider.

PROCEDURE

Fluid aspiration and steroid injection of the right knee.

The risks, benefits, and alternatives were explained to the patient. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.

The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach. 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then blood-tinged fluid was obtained. The right knee was prepped in a standard-fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg of triamcinolone, and 1 mL of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well, with no complications. Post-procedure instructions were given. The patient will follow up with his primary care provider. }}<|eot_id|>
