summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Rodriguez is a pleasant 32-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific event that caused the pain. She describes the pain as a deep achy pain behind her kneecaps. She notes that the pain is exacerbated with getting from a seated position to standing, walking up and down stairs, and getting up from her desk. She notes that the pain improves with rest. She has tried ice and ace wraps. She also takes ibuprofen and aleve, but she tries to avoid taking any medications. She is an active runner and notes that she has decreased her mileage from 5-6 miles to 0.5 miles due to her knee pain. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. She denies a history of any previous knee injuries or broken bones.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that she is employed from home and spends a lot of time at her desk. She is an avid runner.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that she takes ibuprofen and aleve.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.
Skin: Denies redness or swelling.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.

MSK: Examination of the lower extremities: Gait is normal. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. Strength is 5/5 for the remainder of the lower extremities. There is no overlying erythema or ecchymosis. There is no warmth of the skin appreciated. There is no effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.

NEURO: Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

RESULTS

Bilateral knee x-rays were reviewed and reveal no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's history and physical examination, as well as the results of her bilateral knee x-rays, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her symptoms. Her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see in young, active individuals. This condition is caused by the way the kneecap moves along the groove of the thigh bone. I have recommended that she continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with her pain. I have also recommended that she follow up with physical therapy. They will show her a number of lower extremity exercises that will help increase her lower extremity strength, her mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have. She was advised to take it easy from running for now.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>
