HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Susan Smith is a 26-year-old female who presents for a high blood pressure check. The patient reports that she has struggled with high blood pressure in the past, but it has been getting out of control lately. She wishes to know if there is any new medication that she can be on to help get her blood pressure under control, as well as any dietary restrictions she should be taking.

The patient is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily, which was increased from 5 mg daily. Her blood pressures at home have been running high, and she has had headaches. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.

The patient has a history of kidney transplant, which was done a few years ago. She states that everything is stable and she is compliant with her immunosuppressive medications.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or fatigue.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea. Positive for headaches.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Neurological: Denies focal weakness.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• General: The patient is in no distress.
• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm, 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.
• Musculoskeletal: No lower extremity edema. Scar on the left flank for kidney transplant.

RESULTS

• Creatinine: Stable.
• EKG: Normal.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

High blood pressure.

The patient is a 26-year-old female with a history of kidney transplant who presents for a high blood pressure check. She is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily, which was increased from 5 mg daily. Her blood pressures at home have been running high, and she has had headaches. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.

She is currently in stable renal function after her transplant. Her EKG is normal, and her lipid panel and CMP are pending.

I have recommended that we start her on carvedilol 25 mg twice daily. The patient is currently compliant with her immunosuppressive medications. I have also recommended that we obtain a lipid panel and a CMP to rule out any underlying causes of her hypertension.

