summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Christopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of adult hydrocephalus. He was last seen on 10/15/2020 for shunt surgery. He underwent placement of a Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventriculo-peritoneal configuration at a level of 2.0.

The patient reports that he has been doing well since his shunt surgery. He has noticed an improvement in his walking, as he is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance. His bladder control has improved, but has since plateaued, and he is now wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from the diaper. He denies decreased or painful urination. He reports that his cognitive impairment has also improved, and he is able to have normal conversations without difficulty.

The patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He denies any pain in his belly. He denies any recent hospitalizations.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Adult hydrocephalus.

SURGICAL HISTORY

Shunt surgery 8/1/2020.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient lives with his daughter, Theresa.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports that his sister has been very supportive and has been able to help him with his daily activities.

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

None.

VITALS

Blood pressure is 124/80. Heart rate is 64. Respirations are 18. Weight is 174 pounds. Pain is 0 out of 10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Significant for impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Significant for urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Significant for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

RESULTS

Ros is constitutional significant for impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss. GU is significant for urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output. Neurological significant for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucations. Psychiatric is negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.
Vitals: Blood pressure is 124/80, heart rate is 64, respirations are 18, weight is 174 pounds, and pain is 0 out of 10.
Mr. Watson is a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily though his daughter does add some details. The shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.0. He was tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge. He scored a 26 out of 30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25 out of 30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get 2 of the 3 memory words with cuing and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling. At the time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only one with multiple choice.
Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.
Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarchria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no-finger to nose. Of note, the CT scan from the head of 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading there is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus.
Gait impairment.
Urinary incontinence and urgency.
Cognitive impairment.

PLAN

I have followed Mr. Christopher Watson since May 2020 when he first presented with 8 to 10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a spina catheter protocol in June 2020, and he underwent shunt surgery on 8/1/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed at a level of 2.0.

Mr. Watson has been doing well since his last shunt surgery. His walking has improved, as he is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance. His bladder control has improved but has since plateaued, and he is now wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from the diaper. He denies decreased or painful urination. He reports that his cognitive impairment has also improved, and he is able to have normal conversations without difficulty.

The patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He denies any pain in his belly. He denies any recent hospitalizations.

Given his improvement, I believe it is best to continue to monitor his progress over the next 3 months. I do note that there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection on his most recent CT scan. I do not believe this is causing any symptoms and it was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2 to 3 months before we go in changing it. Like I said, if you're not having any headaches or pain and you have improved, that's all good for me. So really there is nothing to worry about.

The patient had a question about driving. I told him that I knew he was going to ask me that one. I think at this point it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver's safety evaluation. Now, I will say that I do occasionally have patients who surprise me by improving over a 9 to 12-month range. And that definitely could be him. He could definitely pass it at that point. So he could definitely enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. So he could kind of do that preemptively and then we could continue to watch for his improvement as we go.

The patient is very cooperative and is doing well. I believe that his improvement will continue. }}<|eot_id|>
