HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Johnson is a pleasant 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. The patient describes the pain as a squeezing, severe pain that is located on the right-hand side of his belly, just below his ribs. The pain is associated with nausea and vomiting. The patient rates his pain as an 8 out of 10. He states that his pain comes and goes. The pain is more severe when he eats, both immediately after and a couple of hours later. He also notes that the pain is worse when he eats fatty foods. The patient has a history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. He states that his diet was worse when he was first diagnosed with diabetes. However, since then, he has cut back on sugar and started a low-carb diet, which he has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. The patient notes that his symptoms have improved with his dietary changes, but he still has pain. He denies any fever, chills, or weight loss. He does have a family history of gallbladder disease as both his father and mother had their gallbladders removed.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.
• Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea, vomiting, and right upper quadrant pain.
• Endocrine: Reports a history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopy, rubs, or murmurs.
• Abdomen: Bowel sounds present. Slight guarding in the right upper quadrant. Positive Murphy's sign. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones in the gallbladder. There is mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
• The patient has not had any improvement with dietary modifications. I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I discussed the risks and benefits of the procedure with the patient today. He understands and agrees with the recommended treatment plan. The procedure will take approximately 1-2 hours and he will spend the night in the hospital for post-operative monitoring. He will follow up with me in 1 week. We will avoid high fat foods postoperatively and he should eat high fiber foods.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
