Neck
• Examination of Neck: Bony protuberance of C5-C6.

Lumbar Spine
• Examination of Lumbosacral Spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.

Musculoskeletal
• Examination of the Musculoskeletal System: Lower extremities: Strength is equal bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the lumbar spine was obtained and reviewed. It demonstrated no acute bony abnormality.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Jeremy Smith is a 56-year-old male who presents for the evaluation of lower back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago when he was moving boxes for his son's college dorm. The patient reports that he felt a sudden onset of pain in his lower back. The pain is sharp and radiates into his right lower extremity. He denies any difficulty or weakness in his legs. The patient reports that he had numbness in his right toes, which resolved after 1 hour. He also states that he had difficulty ambulating for a short period of time. The patient notes that he felt like he was going to topple over because he could not support his upper body weight. The pain is worse when he sits and turns a certain way or when he rolls over in his sleep. The patient denies any incontinence. He has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen, but they do not provide him with any relief. He also states that he tried heat and ice, but they also did not help. The patient denies any gastrointestinal upset from taking ibuprofen. He has had no urgent care or emergency room visits, nor has he had any imaging performed. He was advised to get an MRI, but he was concerned about his insurance coverage. His x-rays were reviewed and demonstrated no acute bony abnormality.

Medical Reasoning:
The patient's symptoms are consistent with a lumbar disk herniation. His x-rays demonstrated no acute bony abnormality. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to further evaluate this.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will proceed with an MRI of the lumbar spine. I have also referred him to physical therapy through our health system. I have also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with pain. If he has a herniated disk, we can discuss other treatment options such as an epidural steroid injection to help reduce the inflammation. }}<|eot_id|>
