ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Shortness of breath.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports shortness of breath and coughing up blood. He has a history of asthma. His oxygen saturation is 94% on room air. Auscultation of the lungs reveals diminished breath sounds bilaterally with rales and rhonchi on the right side. He has a history of a 2/6 ejection murmur which is unchanged from prior.
- Medical Treatment: I have ordered a chest x-ray to evaluate for pneumonia. I also have referred the patient to the emergency department for further evaluation of his shortness of breath and coughing up blood. Blood work including CBC, CMP, PT/PTT will be ordered in the emergency room. A CT chest will also be ordered.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that his shortness of breath could be due to a variety of reasons. I explained that we will need to get a chest x-ray and have him seen in the emergency room for further evaluation. He was advised to hold his Norvasc until his blood pressure is stable.

2. Diabetes.
- Medical Reasoning: His blood glucose levels have been running high. His most recent HbA1c was 7.7.
- Medical Treatment: I have ordered another HbA1c today. I will increase his metformin from 500 mg twice a day to 750 mg twice a day. We will have him follow up in 3 weeks to discuss his diabetes treatment plan.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that his blood glucose levels are high. I advised him to avoid sugary foods and to exercise regularly. I also advised him to monitor his blood glucose levels and to come in if it becomes elevated.

3. Hypertension.
- Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure is running low at home.
- Medical Treatment: He will hold off on his Norvasc until his blood pressure is stable.
