summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Martinez is a pleasant 28-year-old female who presents to the clinic for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. The patient reports a deep, aching pain behind her kneecaps. She notes that the pain is worse with ambulation and activities that cause her to go from a seated position to standing. She states that the pain improves with rest. The patient has tried to ice the area and wrap it with an ace bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or aleve as needed for pain. She denies taking any other medications. The patient reports that she is active and tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running 5-6 miles a day, however, she can only get to half a mile before her leg starts to hurt. She notes that she runs on a treadmill during the winter, but prefers to run outdoors. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. The patient denies any history of knee injuries, but notes that she broke her arm when she was in 6th grade.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain. Denies swelling or redness in the knees.

VITALS

Reviewed today in office. All vital signs are normal.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.

MSK: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of lower extremities are 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema or ecchymosis. No warmth appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. The patient is able to squat, but in pain. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the bilateral knees taken in office today. No fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see in active young individuals. This condition is related to the way the kneecap moves along the groove of the thigh bone. For the pain, she may continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I am also going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises. This will help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have. She will follow up with me in 2 weeks.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. She will continue to take ibuprofen as needed for pain. She will follow the physical therapy regimen. She should avoid running until further notice. }}<|eot_id|>
