• Musculoskeletal: No swelling. Full range of motion.
• Neurological: No focal deficits. Normal reflexes and sensation.
• Extremities: No carotid bruits. No thyromegaly. No edema. Equal pulses.

RESULTS

Hemoglobin A1c: Elevated.

VITALS

Blood pressure: Normal.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Beverly is a 24-year-old female who presents today for a yearly exam. She has a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension.

• Diabetes.
• Medical Reasoning: Her blood sugar levels have been elevated, which is consistent with her hemoglobin A1c being elevated. She has been taking her metformin 500 mg twice daily as prescribed, which has not changed. She has been trying to monitor her blood sugars but has not been successful.
• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily. Encourage the patient to monitor her blood sugars and to call them in in about 2 weeks. If her first fasting blood sugar is elevated, we will come back in about 2 months and reassess. If her blood sugars remain elevated, we will discuss adding another medication.

• Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure has been stable. She has been doing well on her current regimen.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 10 mg once daily.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>
