summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Asthma, allergic rhinitis, food allergy.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Emily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents for a follow-up visit today for asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy.

The patient reports that she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela. She uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall for her seasonal allergy symptoms, which she states work well for her. She also reports that she has a known peanut allergy and that she tolerates almonds and cashews.

The patient reports that she is doing well. She states that she has been using her Wixela as prescribed, 1 puff in the morning and 1 puff at night. She rarely uses her albuterol inhaler, typically only before strenuous activity. She states that she has not had any urgent care or ER visits for breathing issues over the past year.

Regarding her allergies, she states that she uses Zyrtec as needed in the spring and fall. She reports that she does not need it as much in the summer.

The patient reports that she has been trying to cut back on her vaping and smoking, but notes that she still uses them a little bit. She is trying to quit. She notes that she had a history of vaping and smoking, but has been trying to cut back.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
• Asthma, allergic rhinitis, food allergy.

SURGICAL HISTORY

None.

SOCIAL HISTORY

• Patient reports that she is trying to cut back on her vaping and smoking.

FAMILY HISTORY

• Mother with asthma.

MEDICATIONS

• Wixela, 1 puff in the morning and 1 puff at night.
• Zyrtec as needed.
• Albuterol as needed.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, cough, wheezing.
• Skin: Denies rashes or hives.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Ears: No earwax.
• Mouth: No abnormalities noted.
• Nose: No nasal congestion or discharge.
• Heart: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.
• Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.

RESULTS

Spirometry results from today show that the patient's FEV1 is 87% of predicted, FEV1/FVC is 84%, and ACT is 21. These results are similar to her results from 2019.

ASSESSMENT

• Asthma.
• Allergic rhinitis.
• Food allergy.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's spirometry results and discussing her current asthma regimen, I have recommended that we try to reduce her asthma medication to a single inhaler. We discussed that she is currently on Wixela, which is a combination inhaler containing fluticasone and salmeterol. She is currently using it once in the morning and once at night. I recommended that we try stepping down to Alvesco, which is an inhaler containing fluticasone, which she can use 2 puffs twice a day. I have called in a prescription for this to the pharmacy. She will discontinue use of the Wixela. She will continue to use her albuterol as needed. We will repeat her spirometry in 4 months.

I have also recommended that she continue to use Zyrtec as needed for her seasonal allergies, and that she continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 4 months. }}<|eot_id|>
