summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his hypertension. He was recently seen in the emergency room for his blood pressure and palpitations. He notes that he has been feeling fine since then. He denies feeling any palpitations since that time. He attributes his blood pressure to a panic attack which occurred in March 2021 after consuming multiple cups of coffee and no breakfast. He has a history of hypertension since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He reports that he has been taking his Benicar HCT 40-25 and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He reports that he checks his blood pressure daily and notes that it has been running between 146/155.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi noted.

Cardiovascular: Heart sounds are regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.

Musculoskeletal: No edema noted in the bilateral lower extremities.

RESULTS

Thyroid profile from 03/01/2021: Normal.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Resistant hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: Mr. Roberts presents with resistant hypertension. He is on three medications and at maximum dose. His blood pressure is not controlled. I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium levels.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the risks and benefits of the Spironolactone with the patient. I advised him that it can cause gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances.
• Medical Treatment: I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks.
• Additional Testing: We will order a metabolic panel and repeat his blood pressure.
• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

Follow-up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
