summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Irritable bowel syndrome with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that she is a 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 for flares of abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever, chills.
Gastrointestinal: Reports diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.
Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, visual changes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Alert and cooperative, appears stated age.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly, normal active bowel sounds.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 for flares of abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.

She reports that she is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She did have some fried chicken today, though, and was fine for the most part.

I have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications, as well as starting a neuromodulator such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I have asked her to follow up in one month by emailing over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.

INSTRUCTIONS

I have gone ahead and sent her prescriptions to her pharmacy. Just give us a call if she has any questions or need us for anything else. And then let's plan on touching base through MyChart in about a month to just check in to see how she's doing with symptoms. }}<|eot_id|>
