summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.

The patient reports that he has not been sick or been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. The patient states that his walking has improved, he is now able to walk to the dining room just fine, but he still has times where he needs assistance. He also notes that his bladder control did improve after surgery but has leveled out and he is wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper or decreased or painful urination. He also denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He denies any pain in his belly.

The patient states that he is doing better and feels like he can have a normal conversation without feeling lost. He also notes that his daughter, Theresa, summarizes it best saying, "I feel like I can have a normal conversation with him again."

PAST HISTORY

Medical
• Adult hydrocephalus.

SURGICAL HISTORY

• Shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.

MEDICATIONS

• None.

SOCIAL HISTORY

• The patient reports that he is retired and lives with his daughter.

FAMILY HISTORY

• The patient reports that he has a family history of dementia.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Reports continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80.
Heart rate: 64.
Respirations: 18.
Weight: 174 pounds.
Pain: 0 out of 10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: The patient is pleasant and cooperative and able to converse easily through his daughter who adds some details.
Gait: Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.
Neurological: Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor: Normal for bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow from non-nose to finger.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.

RESULTS

CT of the head on 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extraaxial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus.
Gait impairment.
Urinary incontinence and urgency.
Cognitive impairment.

PLAN

I discussed the results of his recent CT scan with the patient today. I have recommended that we continue to monitor him and not change his shunt at this time. We will follow up in 3 months.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
