summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. She has a history of significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2022, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports that things have been going okay. She is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that she had mentioned last time, but they did not get to discuss it too much.

The patient reports that she last drank on Monday. She has been drinking for a while. She reports drinking four to five times per week, hard alcohol.

The patient reports that she is taking her blood pressure medications. She is only taking half of the lisinopril. She reports that the first time she took it she felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She felt much better and did not have the headaches or the “swooshing” that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping, but she does not know if it is okay until she stops drinking.

The patient reports that she is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She reports that she is taking the medicine in the morning when she gets up instead of in the evening. She reports that she is checking her blood pressure at home. She reports that in the morning she is seeing lower numbers than in the evening, where she runs anywhere from 130 to 145/95 to 100. She reports that in the evenings if she sits there for a while and then takes it, it goes down. Kind of like when she comes here it is always high, but then it goes down. In the mornings it is generally more normal, where it is supposed to be.

The patient reports that she had received the COVID-19 vaccine on 02/09/2022.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, weight loss.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, syncope, dyspnea on exertion.
• Respiratory: Denies cough, sputum production, wheezing, shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, muscle aches.
• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, numbness or tingling, weakness.
• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, hematuria, frequency, urgency.
• Dermatologic: Denies rashes, pruritus, itching.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Constitutional: Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress. Well-nourished, well-developed, no signs of dehydration.
• Skin: Moist, good turgor.
• HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. PERRLA, extraocular movements intact, no focal deficits. Neck: Supple, no thyromegaly, JVD, no bruits. Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, unlabored breathing. Cardiac: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, gallops. Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, non-distended, bowel sounds present. Extremities: No edema, good peripheral pulses, no cyanosis, clubbing, or trophic changes. Neurological: Alert and oriented to person, place, time, and situation. Strength 5/5 in all extremities, sensation intact, reflexes 2+ and equal bilaterally, no Babinski, no Hoffman's.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Alcohol use disorder: She is drinking alcohol four to five days per week to excess. No tobacco, no substance abuse. Skin is moist, good turgor. On exam, she is alert, pleasant, in no acute distress. She does not appear anxious, agitated, or depressed. She would like to start Naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2022. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Her target BP is 130/140.
• Hypertension: She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol.
• Chronic conditions: We will see her back in one month, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. I recommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix. She did defer this.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will see her back in one month, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. }}<|eot_id|>
