summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Rash.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a pleasant 45-year-old female who presents today for follow up of her perioral dermatitis. She reports that her rash is doing much better. She notes that she started using doxycycline once daily as she thought the pharmacist recommended. She states she was taking it twice daily for the first two weeks and then changed to once daily and she noticed improvement. However, it plateaued a little bit and she is now using it once daily. She is not breastfeeding at this time.

The patient denies the use of Elidel. The patient states she stopped breastfeeding and then requested the doxycycline at that time. She was advised that even with doxycycline, she can keep using the Elidel. The patient states she did not take the Elidel because she got worried when she read about it. The doctor explained that many of the things she read about is not actually relevant to the cream or ointment form but it is about the oral form that is used in really high doses after people have a heart transplant or a kidney transplant or something like that. She was advised that doxycycline is low risk.

The patient reports she is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily. She denies that it is drying her out too much. She reports she uses the metrocream after her face wash. She denies the use of Cetaphil cleanser, Neutrogena wipes, and any lotions.

The patient reports that she went down to just taking the doxycycline once a day last week. She states that she started seeing improvement and then changed to just once a day.

The patient has Fitzpatrick skin type IV. She is not going to burn, she is going to tan. She is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few really faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath her nostrils. She is right, it is not totally gone.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that she has refills available for her doxycycline.

PHYSICAL EXAM

The patient's Fitzpatrick skin type is IV. She is not going to burn, she is going to tan. She is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few really faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath her nostrils. She is right, it is not totally gone.

ASSESSMENT

Perioral dermatitis. Rosacea, including ocular rosacea.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's history and physical, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current rash. She is currently using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily and metrocream. I recommended that she increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. She will continue to use the sulfacetamide wash and metrocream. She will apply the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is resolved. She will then use the ointment once daily for another week before stopping. She will continue to use the face wash and metrocream.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in four weeks. If the rash starts to flare again, she can try just doing the tacrolimus ointment for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then she can restart the doxycycline as well.

The patient has a refill available for her doxycycline. She will cancel her Elidel cream prescription. I will also cancel her clindamycin prescription. I recommended that she try using a basic moisturizer such as Vanicream or petroleum jelly. She should stay away from any anti-aging creams, any plant creams. She should reach out to me if she is going to try something else. I gave her a couple of samples. }}<|eot_id|>
