Pain in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Johnson is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of pain in the right upper quadrant of his abdomen. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago, and it is intermittent. He describes the pain as severe and located underneath his ribs on the right hand side. He also experiences nausea and occasional vomiting. The pain is worse after meals and lasts for several hours. He denies any fever. The patient has been avoiding high-fat foods, as he was advised by Dr. Miller, but his symptoms have not improved.

The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been managing it with dietary changes and exercise. He reports that he has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months and has been following a ketogenic diet.

He denies any family history of gallbladder disease.

PAST HISTORY

Medical: Type 2 diabetes, gallbladder disease.
Surgical: Cholecystectomy.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he enjoys photography and hiking.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he is currently taking metformin.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers.
Gastrointestinal: Endorses pain, nausea, vomiting.
Endocrine: Endorses weight loss.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

Respiratory: Clear and equal bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
Abdomen: Soft, no distention. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding in the right upper quadrant. Positive Murphy's sign.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall noted. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with intermittent right upper quadrant pain that is associated with nausea and occasional vomiting. The patient has avoided high-fat foods, but his symptoms have not improved. His abdominal ultrasound revealed multiple gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.
• Medical Treatment: We discussed the nature of his symptoms and the results of his abdominal ultrasound. Given his symptoms and imaging findings, I have recommended that we proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I explained to the patient that the procedure is expected to take about 1 hour to 1.5 hours to complete and he will likely spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. I advised him to avoid high-fat foods after surgery and to eat foods that are high in fiber.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to follow up with his primary care physician to monitor his type 2 diabetes, especially in light of the upcoming surgery. He will also continue to follow up with his endocrinologist or primary care provider for his diabetes management. }}<|eot_id|>
