CV: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.

MSK: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities are 5/5. On knee exam, there is no overlying erythema or ecchymosis. There is no warmth of the skin appreciated. There is no effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Negative Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests bilaterally.

RESULTS

Bilateral knee x-rays: No fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bilateral knee pain.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient's symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a common condition that we see, especially in active young people. It has to do with the way the patient's kneecap moves along the groove of her thigh.
- Medical Treatment: Continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I am going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises. This will help increase her lower extremity strength, her mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have.

