HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Smith is a pleasant 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of his pain began two weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books. He states that he felt a "pop" at that time and since then he has been experiencing low back pain. The pain is described as achy and sharp in nature and is localized to his low back. The patient notes that the pain has radiated down his left leg. He denies any numbness or tingling associated with the pain. He denies any loss of sensation or weakness. He denies any loss of bladder or bowel control.

The patient states that the pain has worsened over time. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol, which sometimes helps. He denies any history of surgery.

The patient is an Olympic weightlifter with books and tries to exercise a few times per week. He notes that he is sedentary at work. He denies that the pain has affected his job.

The patient states that his father has a history of back pain. He denies a history of playing any sports. He states that he is a fan of the Tatum Hotspur. He denies that he smokes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.

Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.

Musculoskeletal: On examination of the back, there is no overlying redness or bruising on the skin. Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Pain with lumbar extension. Supine straight leg test is positive.

RESULTS

X-ray of the lumbar spine taken today is unremarkable.

RESULTS

Magnetic resonance imaging of the lumbar spine performed today demonstrates disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Lumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to herniated disk with radiculopathy.

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with him regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended that we proceed with a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and a corticosteroid. I have explained to the patient that this is a typical procedure that I do all the time. It is in-office and we can get him scheduled today if he would like. I have advised the patient that there are risks associated with this type of injection, just like any procedure, but we aim to minimize them.

INSTRUCTIONS

