Adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents for a follow-up visit. The patient is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa. The patient underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020, for the treatment of adult hydrocephalus. He was previously seen in the clinic in May 2020, when he initially presented with an 8-10 year history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. The patient underwent a Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in a ventricular peritoneal configuration, which was programmed at level 2.0, and was placed.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery. He notes that his walking has improved and he is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance. However, he still has times when he needs assistance. His bladder control has leveled out, and he continues to wear diapers. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper or decreased or painful urination.

The patient reports that his cognitive abilities have also improved. He states that he does not feel lost when talking to someone anymore. His daughter reports that his sister, Hannah, summarized it best by stating, "I feel like I can have a normal conversation with him again." The patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He also denies any pain in his belly.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that he has not been taking any new medications.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Denies constipation or diarrhea.
Genitourinary: Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

