Pain in right upper abdomen.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Smith is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents today for evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The pain has been present for several weeks and is intermittent. The patient states that the pain is severe and comes and goes. The pain is located in the right upper abdomen and is described as a squeezing pain. He rates the pain as an 8/10. The patient notes that the pain is worse after meals and has been associated with nausea and vomiting. The patient states that he has had a family history of gallbladder disease as both of his parents had their gallbladders removed. He denies any fever, chills, or recent weight loss.

The patient is a type 2 diabetic and was previously advised to modify his diet to manage his blood sugars. The patient states that he has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. He notes that his blood sugars have improved, but he is still experiencing pain.

The patient denies any recent travel, fever, chills, or recent weight loss. He reports that he is able to continue his normal activities.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking medications for his diabetes.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Reports occasional fevers and nausea.
• Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports pain in the right upper abdomen.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• General: Appears well.
• HEENT: No abnormalities noted.
• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
• Pulmonary: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Abdomen: Soft, nontender, nondistended. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding noted to the right upper quadrant. Murphy's sign is positive.
• Extremities: No clubbing, cyanosis, or edema.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.

The patient was advised that since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, we will proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. He was advised to avoid high fat foods after surgery and to eat foods high in fiber. He was also advised to follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes and continue with his current medications.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will have a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. He will be seen in one week postoperatively and will be released from the hospital after the procedure. He will need to avoid high fat foods and eat foods high in fiber after surgery. He will also follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
