summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Rodriguez is a pleasant 35-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left knee pain. The patient reports that she injured her left knee about a week ago while running around with her 5-year-old niece who is learning to ride a bike without training wheels. The patient states that she heard a pop and felt pain in her knee. She has been limping since the injury and has not been able to run. She rates her pain as a 4 out of 10 and states it is worse at night. She denies any numbness or tingling in her lower extremity. The patient reports that she has been taking Tylenol 500 mg every 4 hours as needed for pain. She denies taking any NSAIDs. The patient states that she has been wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient has a past medical history significant for an ulcer. She reports that her primary care physician advised her to avoid taking NSAIDs due to her history of an ulcer.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the left knee: Examination of the left knee reveals 2+ edema, moderate effusion. Range of motion is limited. Positive pain to palpation of the medial aspect of the knee. Negative Lachman's test and negative valgus and varus stress testing.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee was reviewed today and is unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee strain.

PLAN

I have discussed my assessment and plan with the patient. She will be placed in a knee immobilizer and advised to avoid running for 3 weeks. She will continue to take Tylenol as needed. I will prescribe Tramadol 50 mg twice a day. She will ice her knee 3-4 times a day for 20-30 minutes at a time. I will have her follow up with me in 2 weeks to review her progress.

Patient understanding and agreement: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
